Wireshark is a network protocol analyzer used to capture and inspect network traffic.

1. Packet capture using wireshark
   i) Open wireshark
   ii) Select the active network interface (Wi-Fi/Ethernet).
   iii) Click start capturing packets icon.
   iv) Capture the network packets for few seconds.
   v) Generate network traffic by opening a website or using the ping command.(Ping command - ping www.google.com)
   If ping command not found - sudo apt update , sudo apt install iputils-ping
   vi) We get an ip address from google.com. Type the ip address in the filter pane (ip.addr == <IP Address> in wireshark and click enter.
   vii) Observe the captured packets.
   viii) Click the Stop button to stop capturing.
   ix) Save the captured packets using File → Save As.
2. Starting Wireshark
   i) Install Wireshark on the system.
   ii) Open Wireshark from the Start menu.
   iii) The main screen displays available network interfaces.
   iv) Select an interface such as Wi-Fi or Ethernet.
   v) Double-click the interface to start capturing packets.
   vi) Click the red Stop button to stop the capture.
3. Viewing captured traffic
   i) There are 3 sections in wireshark - Packet List, Packet Details, Packet Bytes.
   ii) Important packet fields : 
       No.: Packet number.
       Time: Time at which the packet was captured.
       Source: IP address of the sender.
       Destination: IP address of the receiver.
       Protocol: Protocol used, such as TCP, UDP, or ICMP.
       Length: Size of the packet.
       Info: Additional packet information.
   iii) Observe the captured packets in the Packet List pane.
   iv) Click a packet to view its details.
   v) Examine the Packet Details and Packet Bytes panes.
   vi) Double-click a packet to open it in a new window for detailed analysis.
4. Analysis, Statistics and Filters
   i) For analysing captured traffic, wireshark provides statistical tools.
   ii) Statistics option	              Purpose
       Protocol Hierarchy	              Displays protocols present in the captured traffic.
       Conversations	                  Shows communication between two endpoints.
       Endpoints	                      Lists unique IP and MAC addresses.
       I/O Graphs                     	Displays network traffic over time.
       Flow Graph	                      Shows the flow of packets between devices.
       Capture File Properties	        Displays information about the capture file.
    iii) Filters
        Filter	                    Meaning
        icmp	                     Capture and Display ICMP packets.
        tcp	                       Capture and Display TCP packets.
        udp	                       Capture and Display UDP packets.
        dns	                       Capture and Display DNS packets.
        http	                     Capture and Display HTTP packets.
        port 80	                   Capture packets using port 80.
        host 192.168.1.1	         Capture packets to or from the specified host.
        ip.addr == 192.168.1.1	   Display packets to or from the IP address.
        tcp.port == 80	           Display TCP packets using port 80.
        ip.src == 192.168.1.1	     Display packets from the specified source IP.
        ip.dst == 192.168.1.1	     Display packets to the specified destination IP.
